    <a class="back" href="/tutorials/">← Back to tutorials</a><div class="eyebrow">Tutorials / Electrical / Tools</div><h1>{title}</h1>
    <p class="dek">A digital multimeter can look intimidating: there is a dial full of symbols, a display, and more than one place to plug in the probes. The basic workflow gets much easier when you ask three things: <strong>What am I measuring? How should the meter be set? Where do the probes go?</strong></p>
    <div class="byline">Published September 23, 2026 · Workshop Girl</div><ShareButton title={title} canonical={canonical} label="Share this article" idSuffix="intro" />
    <figure class="hero"><img src="/images/tutorials/how-to-use-a-multimeter/multimeter-basics-workshopgirl.webp" alt="WorkshopGirl learning the controls and test leads of a digital multimeter" width="1672" height="941" fetchpriority="high" /><figcaption>Start with the meter’s labels and the job you want to check. The exact dial and jack layout differs by model.</figcaption></figure>

    <article>
      <h2>Know the basic parts of your multimeter</h2>
      <p>Most digital multimeters have an LCD display, a rotary selector dial and two test leads. The probes at the ends of the leads touch the test points. The sockets at the bottom of the meter are called jacks or inputs.</p>
      <div class="parts-grid"><div class="part"><strong>LCD display</strong><p>Shows the measurement and unit, such as V, Ω or a continuity symbol. “OL” often means over range or an open path, depending on mode.</p></div><div class="part"><strong>Rotary selector</strong><p>Chooses the measurement function. Some meters combine multiple functions on one dial position.</p></div><div class="part"><strong>Black lead → COM</strong><p>COM means common. Plug the black lead here for the checks in this guide.</p></div><div class="part"><strong>Red lead → V/Ω input</strong><p>Use the jack marked V, VΩ or similar for voltage, resistance and continuity.</p></div><div class="part"><strong>Current / amp input</strong><p>A separate A or mA jack may be provided for current tests. It is not the input for the measurements below.</p></div></div>
      <p>For the voltage, resistance and continuity checks here, the setup is <strong>black lead in COM; red lead in the voltage/resistance jack</strong>. Jack names and layouts vary, so read your meter’s markings and manual. Before measuring, confirm the red lead is not still plugged into a high-current A or mA input. A lead in the wrong jack can create a short circuit if used across a battery.</p>

      <aside class="quick"><h2>Quick answer: what are you checking?</h2><div class="quick-grid"><div class="quick-card"><strong>Voltage (V)</strong><p>Electrical potential difference between two points. A suitable low-voltage circuit may be energized for this test.</p></div><div class="quick-card"><strong>Resistance (Ω)</strong><p>How much a component or path opposes current. Turn power off and isolate the part before measuring.</p></div><div class="quick-card"><strong>Continuity</strong><p>A quick check for a low-resistance path. Test an unpowered, isolated wire or component.</p></div></div><p><strong>Key safety difference:</strong> voltage is checked across an energized source when the procedure and meter are appropriate. Resistance and continuity are normally checked with power off.</p></aside>

      <h2>Understanding the dial</h2>
      <p>Symbols differ a little between meters, but these common markings are useful:</p>
      <ul><li><strong>V⎓ or V with a solid and dashed line:</strong> DC voltage, the kind supplied by batteries.</li><li><strong>V~:</strong> AC voltage, which changes direction periodically. This guide does not teach household mains testing.</li><li><strong>Ω:</strong> resistance, measured in ohms.</li><li><strong>Sound-wave or diode/beep symbol:</strong> often continuity mode; some meters share a dial position with resistance.</li><li><strong>A, mA or µA:</strong> current ranges. Current measurement uses a different connection method and is outside this beginner guide.</li></ul>
      <p>An <strong>autoranging</strong> meter selects a suitable range for you. A manual-ranging meter asks you to choose one; for an unfamiliar voltage, select a range above what you expect and step down if needed. Check the manual rather than guessing when the display or symbols are unfamiliar.</p>

      <h2>Three common checks at a glance</h2>
      <div class="measure-grid" aria-label="Multimeter measurement reference">
        <section class="measure-card"><h3>Voltage</h3><dl><dt>Typical mode</dt><dd>V DC or V AC, as appropriate</dd><dt>Circuit state</dt><dd>May be energized for a suitable low-voltage check</dd><dt>Probe connection</dt><dd>Across the two points</dd></dl></section>
        <section class="measure-card"><h3>Resistance</h3><dl><dt>Typical mode</dt><dd>Ω</dd><dt>Circuit state</dt><dd>Power off; component isolated as needed</dd><dt>Probe connection</dt><dd>Across the component or path</dd></dl></section>
        <section class="measure-card"><h3>Continuity</h3><dl><dt>Typical mode</dt><dd>Continuity / sound-wave symbol</dd><dt>Circuit state</dt><dd>Power off; component isolated</dd><dt>Probe connection</dt><dd>One probe at each end of the path</dd></dl></section>
      </div>

      <h2>How to measure DC voltage</h2>
      <p>Voltage is measured <strong>across</strong> two points. A conventional 12V car battery is a useful low-voltage example. This check shows the voltage at rest; it is not a complete battery health test.</p>
      <figure><img src="/images/tutorials/how-to-use-a-multimeter/measure-car-battery-voltage-multimeter.webp" alt="WorkshopGirl measuring the resting voltage of a 12V car battery with a digital multimeter" width="1672" height="941" loading="lazy" /><figcaption>For DC voltage, place one probe on each battery terminal and read the voltage across them.</figcaption></figure>
      <ol class="steps"><li>Park securely and switch the vehicle and accessories off for a simple resting-voltage check. Keep hands, leads and clothing away from moving parts.</li><li>Put the black lead in <strong>COM</strong> and the red lead in the <strong>V/Ω</strong> jack—not the A or mA jack.</li><li>Set the selector to DC voltage (V⎓ or the meter’s equivalent). Choose a suitable range if the meter is not autoranging.</li><li>Touch the red probe to the battery’s positive (+) terminal and the black probe to the negative (−) terminal. Keep the probe tips from touching each other or bridging the terminals.</li><li>Read the display. If the probes are reversed, many digital meters show a minus sign; reposition them if you want a positive reading.</li></ol>
      <aside class="tip"><h3>Remember: voltage goes across</h3><p>A voltmeter connects across the two points being measured. Do not try to measure current by placing the meter directly across a battery—that can cause a short and damage equipment.</p></aside>
      <p>A rested conventional lead-acid 12V battery may read around <strong>12.6 V</strong> when fully charged under suitable conditions, but that is not a universal pass/fail number. Chemistry, temperature, recent charging or surface charge, state of charge and vehicle conditions all affect interpretation. A voltage reading alone does not establish the battery’s overall condition. For more on the battery itself, see <a href="/tutorials/how-to-replace-a-car-battery/">our beginner car-battery guide</a>.</p>

      <h2>Measuring resistance</h2>
      <p>Resistance, shown in ohms (Ω), describes how strongly a material or component opposes electrical current. Lower resistance generally means an easier path; higher resistance means more opposition. The expected value depends entirely on the component and circuit—there is no one “good” resistance number for everything.</p>
      <p><strong>Power off first.</strong> Switch off and disconnect power to the circuit before measuring resistance. If the part is connected to other components, those parallel paths can affect the reading; isolate at least one side when appropriate. Discharge capacitors according to the equipment’s instructions before testing. Measuring resistance on an energized circuit can give misleading results and may damage the meter or create a hazard.</p>
      <p>For a simple example, disconnect a wire from the circuit at both ends, select Ω, and touch one probe to each end. A low reading suggests a low-resistance path through that wire; a very high reading or “OL” may mean the path is open or outside the selected range. Probe contact, lead resistance and the meter’s range also affect the display.</p>

      <h2>How to perform a continuity test</h2>
      <p>Continuity mode is a quick way to check whether there is a low-resistance electrical path between two points. Many meters beep when the resistance is below that model’s threshold; the threshold is not identical on every meter.</p>
      <figure><img src="/images/tutorials/how-to-use-a-multimeter/continuity-test-automotive-fuse.webp" alt="WorkshopGirl checking continuity across a removed automotive blade fuse with a digital multimeter" width="1672" height="941" loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Check continuity across the conductive contacts of a removed, isolated fuse.</figcaption></figure>
      <aside class="safety"><h2>Remove and isolate the fuse first</h2><p><strong>This continuity test is for a fuse that has been removed from the vehicle and isolated from an energized circuit.</strong> Do not continuity-test an energized circuit.</p></aside>
      <ol class="steps"><li>Switch the vehicle off and remove the fuse using the vehicle maker’s procedure. Keep track of its location and rating.</li><li>Confirm the fuse is out of the circuit and not energized. The fuse itself must be isolated for this check.</li><li>Put the black lead in COM, the red lead in V/Ω and select continuity mode.</li><li>Touch one probe to each conductive contact on the fuse. Avoid touching the metal probe tips unnecessarily.</li><li>Observe the display and listen for the meter’s indication. A beep or low reading generally means a path is present; “OL” or no beep generally means the path is open. Check your meter manual for its specific behavior.</li></ol>
      <p>A continuity beep does <strong>not</strong> prove that every component or the whole circuit is healthy under operating conditions. It is one simple diagnostic check. A fuse can have continuity and still fail under load, while other faults may exist elsewhere in the circuit.</p>

      <h2>Common multimeter mistakes</h2>
      <ul><li>Leaving the red lead in the A or mA jack, then probing a battery in voltage mode.</li><li>Choosing the wrong selector position or mixing up AC and DC.</li><li>Using resistance or continuity mode on an energized circuit.</li><li>Touching exposed probe metal or slipping so the tips short adjacent conductors.</li><li>Assuming a continuity beep proves an entire circuit is healthy.</li><li>Using the meter or leads beyond their ratings—or assuming every meter has the same dial and jack layout.</li></ul>
      <aside class="mistake"><h3>A quick habit that prevents a common error</h3><p>Before every measurement, check the red lead’s jack and the dial setting. For this article’s voltage, resistance and continuity checks, the red lead belongs in V/Ω and the black lead in COM.</p></aside>

      <h2>A simple three-question workflow</h2>
      <div class="workflow-grid"><section class="workflow-card"><strong>1. What am I measuring?</strong><p>Voltage, resistance or continuity?</p></section><section class="workflow-card"><strong>2. Powered or unpowered?</strong><p>Voltage may be checked on a suitable energized low-voltage circuit. Resistance and continuity: power off, isolate as needed.</p></section><section class="workflow-card"><strong>3. Are the leads in the right jacks?</strong><p>Black in COM; red in V/Ω for these checks.</p></section></div>

      <h2>What to look for in a beginner multimeter</h2>
      <p>You do not need every function to learn these basics. Look for a clear display, a dial that labels the modes plainly, DC voltage and resistance ranges, a continuity buzzer, and replaceable test leads. Autoranging can make setup simpler, though a manual-ranging meter can also work when its range is selected correctly.</p>
      <p>A protective case or boot can help with ordinary knocks. Check that the meter and leads have safety ratings suitable for the intended measurement environment, and that the meter’s maximum input ratings are clear. A rating does not make every measurement safe: use the manual, inspect leads for damage and stay within your experience. This guide is about low-voltage beginner checks, not selecting equipment for household wiring.</p>

      <aside class="safety"><h2>Start with low voltage</h2><p>Batteries and isolated low-voltage circuits are better places to learn than household mains wiring. <strong>Do not experiment with household mains voltage as a practice exercise.</strong> Electricity can cause shock, fire, short circuits or equipment damage when the procedure or equipment is inappropriate. If you are unsure about a circuit, stop and get help from someone qualified.</p></aside>

      <aside class="quick-rule"><h2>WorkshopGirl Quick Rule</h2><p><strong>Voltage: across, and only when the setup is appropriate.</strong></p><p><strong>Resistance and continuity: power off, isolated as needed.</strong></p><p>Check the dial, the jacks and your meter’s manual before the probes touch anything.</p></aside>

      <section class="faq" aria-labelledby="faq-title"><h2 id="faq-title">Multimeter FAQ</h2>
        <h3>Where does the black multimeter lead go?</h3><p>For the checks in this guide, plug it into the jack marked COM.</p>
        <h3>Where does the red lead go?</h3><p>For voltage, resistance and continuity, use the jack marked V, VΩ or a similar label. Do not leave it in the high-current input.</p>
        <h3>Can I measure resistance with power on?</h3><p>No. Turn off and disconnect power before measuring resistance or continuity, and discharge capacitors as the equipment instructions specify.</p>
        <h3>Does a continuity beep mean a fuse or wire is good?</h3><p>It generally means the meter detected a low-resistance path at that moment. It does not prove the part or the whole circuit will work under operating conditions.</p>
        <h3>Is 12.6 volts always a good car-battery reading?</h3><p>No. Around 12.6 V can be typical for a fully charged conventional lead-acid battery at rest in suitable conditions, but battery chemistry and test conditions affect the reading. Voltage alone is not a complete health test.</p>
      </section>

      <section class="share-end" aria-labelledby="share-end-title"><h2 id="share-end-title">Share this article</h2><p>Know someone learning basic electrical checks? Send them this beginner guide.</p><ShareButton title={title} canonical={canonical} label="Share this article" idSuffix="end" /></section>

